34|The Quiet Car Ride|车里安静五分钟|出行与办事|一家人坐车出游，Ben的提问不断，大家商量轮流选音乐。|询问剩余时间、轮流选择、提出请求
playlist=播放列表|rest stop=休息站|take turns=轮流
How much longer?|Could we turn it down a little?|Let's take turns choosing songs.
lucy: Little Moments. The Quiet Car Ride. We've been in the car for twenty minutes when Ben asks the question Dad knew was coming.
ben: How much longer?
dad: About an hour. We'll stop for a break on the way.
ben: And how long until the break?
lucy: I look out the window and try not to laugh. Mom opens a playlist on her phone. Music fills the car, which gives Ben something new to talk about.
ben: I don't know this song. Can we play mine?
lucy: Your favorite song is forty seconds long, and we heard it eight times yesterday.
mom: Let's take turns choosing songs. One each, then we start again. I'll handle the phone while Dad drives.
lucy: Ben chooses first. His song has a lot of clapping. I choose a quieter song next, and Dad taps one finger on the steering wheel.
lucy: Could we turn it down a little? I'd like to hear the words without the windows shaking.
mom: Of course. How's that?
lucy: Much better. For a few minutes, nobody says anything. The fields slide past, the music plays softly, and even Ben looks peaceful.
ben: I have a question.
dad: Let me guess. How much longer?
ben: No. Can my next turn be two songs? Mine are very short.
lucy: We all laugh. At the rest stop, we stretch our legs and get some water. Back in the car, I add one extra quiet song to the list. Hope is a beautiful thing.
===
35|The Shoes-Off Question|做客时要脱鞋吗|周末与朋友|第一次到Emma家做客，Lucy先问清楚家里的习惯。|礼貌询问、表示感谢、适应主人习惯
guest=客人|slippers=拖鞋|comfortable=舒服的
Should I take my shoes off?|Make yourself comfortable.|Thanks for having me.
lucy: Little Moments. The Shoes-Off Question. Emma invites me over after school. I've met her at the park and the library, but this is my first visit to her house. At the front door, I notice several pairs of shoes lined up neatly.
lucy: Should I take my shoes off?
emma: Yes, please. We usually leave them here. There are clean slippers in that basket if you'd like a pair.
lucy: Thanks. I put my sneakers beside hers and choose a pair of slippers. They have little clouds on them, which makes walking feel slightly more interesting.
emma: Make yourself comfortable. You can put your backpack by the chair.
lucy: Her room has a shelf full of drawings and a desk covered with colored pencils. I reach toward a tiny clay cat, then stop. May I pick this up?
emma: Sure. Just hold the base. I made it in art class, and one ear is a little delicate.
lucy: I look at it carefully and put it back. We spend the afternoon drawing imaginary pets. Mine is a dog with enormous ears. Emma's is a very serious-looking purple turtle.
emma: Want to stay for a snack? My mom has sliced some fruit.
lucy: That sounds great. Before Dad picks me up, I put the pencils away and leave the slippers by the basket. Thanks for having me.
emma: Come again soon. Your enormous-eared dog needs a friend.
lucy: Next time, I'll bring my sketchbook. The shoes can wait by the door while we invent another impossible animal.
===
36|The Not-So-Lost Remote|遥控器没有走远|家庭日常|大家找不到遥控器，决定按照最后使用的线索逐步寻找。|回忆经过、描述位置、请求挪动物品
remote=遥控器|cushion=靠垫|underneath=在下面
Who had it last?|Could you move over a little?|Let's check one place at a time.
lucy: Little Moments. The Not-So-Lost Remote. We are ready for a nature show. The snacks are ready, the lights are low, and the television is off because the remote has disappeared. Dad checks the coffee table. I check under a magazine. Ben checks his snack bowl.
lucy: Why would it be in your snack bowl?
ben: I like to be thorough.
dad: Who had it last?
lucy: I used it this afternoon. Then I put it on the couch. I think. Maybe beside the blue cushion.
ben: Maybe the couch ate it. It eats my pencils all the time.
dad: Let's check one place at a time. Lucy, look beside the couch. Ben, check under the cushions.
lucy: I find a sock, two building blocks, and a pencil. The couch clearly has hobbies. Ben lifts one cushion, then the next. Still no remote.
ben: Dad, could you move over a little?
dad: Why? Do you think it's underneath me?
lucy: Dad stands up. There, exactly where he was sitting, is the remote. Nobody speaks for a second. Then Ben slowly raises one eyebrow.
ben: We checked almost everywhere except the most suspicious person.
dad: In my defense, it was surprisingly comfortable.
lucy: The television finally turns on. After the show, we put the remote in a small tray on the table. Dad checks his seat before sitting down again. The couch may eat pencils, but tonight we've rescued its biggest snack.
===
37|A Shortcut Through the Menu|看懂陌生的菜单|吃饭与购物|Lucy不认识菜单上的一道菜，直接向服务员问清楚。|询问食材、说明偏好、请求分开放
ingredients=食材|on the side=另外分开放|spicy=辣的
What's in this sandwich?|Is the sauce spicy?|Could I have the sauce on the side?
lucy: Little Moments. A Shortcut Through the Menu. Mom and I try a new sandwich shop. The menu has names like Garden Stack and Sunset Special. They sound beautiful, but I have no idea what I'm supposed to eat.
mom: You can ask. A name doesn't tell you everything.
lucy: I point to the Garden Stack when the server comes over. What's in this sandwich?
clerk: Roasted vegetables, cheese, lettuce, and our house sauce, on toasted bread.
lucy: Is the sauce spicy?
clerk: A little. It has some chili in it. If you prefer, we can put it in a separate little cup.
lucy: Could I have the sauce on the side?
clerk: Absolutely. Anything else you'd like to know?
lucy: No, thank you. That sounds good. Mom orders a different sandwich, and we sit by a shelf of plants. When our food arrives, I try a tiny bit of sauce first.
mom: How is it?
lucy: Good, but a little is enough for me. I add some to one corner of the sandwich instead of pouring it over everything.
mom: Glad you asked?
lucy: Very glad. I would have guessed Garden Stack meant a pile of lettuce with a flower on top.
clerk: Everything okay here?
lucy: Yes, thanks. Your garden is delicious. The server laughs, and Mom smiles over her sandwich. Next time a menu sounds like poetry, I'll ask about the ingredients. It's easier than trying to eat a sunset.
===
38|The Calendar Collision|周六的安排撞车了|家庭日常|两个活动安排在同一时间，Lucy主动联系朋友调整计划。|发现时间冲突、改约、确认新时间
calendar=日历|reschedule=改约|available=有空的
I think I double-booked myself.|Would Sunday work instead?|Let me check my calendar.
lucy: Little Moments. The Calendar Collision. I tell Mom I'm meeting Emma at the park on Saturday morning. Mom looks up from the family calendar with a puzzled expression. That is never a good sign.
mom: Saturday morning? We're helping your aunt move some boxes, remember?
lucy: Oh. I remembered the boxes, but apparently I forgot what day they belonged to. I think I double-booked myself.
mom: It happens. Tell Emma as soon as you can, and see whether another time works.
lucy: I call Emma and explain the problem. I'm sorry. I should have checked before making plans. Would Sunday work instead?
emma: Let me check my calendar. Sunday morning is free, but I have a family lunch at twelve.
lucy: How about nine thirty? We could stay for an hour, and you'd have time to get home.
emma: That works. Same park, by the big slide?
lucy: Yes. I'll put it in my calendar right now. I write down the day, time, and meeting place, then tell Mom about the new plan.
mom: Good. Now we can all see what's happening.
lucy: On Saturday, we help carry light boxes to my aunt's new apartment. On Sunday, I meet Emma exactly where we agreed. She waves from beside the slide.
emma: No surprise boxes today?
lucy: None. I checked. We walk around the park and talk about our weekend. My calendar has two clear plans instead of one impossible morning. It isn't exciting to look at, but it works beautifully.
===
39|The Neighborhood Cleanup|社区清洁小队|周末与朋友|参加社区活动时，Lucy和朋友分工捡拾垃圾，也懂得请大人处理不认识的物品。|报名参与、询问分工、说明发现
gloves=手套|cleanup=清洁活动|team=小队
Where should we start?|I'll hold the bag.|Let's ask an adult about that.
lucy: Little Moments. The Neighborhood Cleanup. On Saturday, Dad takes Emma, Ben, and me to a cleanup at the park. We join the volunteers near the entrance and get gloves, bags, and litter pickers. Ben immediately starts opening and closing his picker like a giant bird beak.
dad: Remember, pick up ordinary litter only. Leave sharp or unfamiliar things alone and show an adult.
lucy: Where should we start?
dad: Along this path. Stay where I can see you, and don't reach into the bushes.
emma: I'll hold the bag. Lucy, you can use the picker. We'll switch in a few minutes.
lucy: We find paper cups, snack wrappers, and a very lonely plastic spoon. Ben spots something shiny near the edge of the path but stops before touching it.
ben: I can't tell what that is.
lucy: Let's ask an adult about that. Dad calls the organizer over, and we leave it for them to handle. Then we return to the easy things on our stretch of path.
emma: This looks better already. You can actually see the grass beside the bench.
ben: I found another spoon. Maybe they're friends.
lucy: After our turn, we hand in the equipment and wash our hands before having a snack. The park isn't magically perfect, but our little section looks much tidier.
dad: Nice work, team.
lucy: Ben gives his empty hands one last imaginary bird-beak snap. Emma and I laugh. It turns out a cleanup can be useful and slightly ridiculous at the same time.
===
40|The Best Part Was Tiny|最喜欢的都是小事|家庭日常|周日晚餐时，每个人分享一件最近让自己开心的小事。|分享经历、追问细节、表达感受
favorite=最喜欢的|moment=时刻|notice=留意
What was your favorite part?|That sounds like a good day.|Mine was something small.
lucy: Little Moments. The Best Part Was Tiny. At dinner on Sunday, Mom asks us a question. She wants to know one good thing from the week. Ben immediately says dessert, even though dessert hasn't happened yet.
mom: Something that already happened, please. Lucy, what was your favorite part?
lucy: Mine was something small. Emma saved me a seat at lunch on a day when I felt tired. She didn't make a big deal of it. She just moved her backpack and smiled.
dad: That sounds like a good day.
lucy: It became a better day. What about you?
dad: I found the screwdriver I'd been looking for. It was exactly where I had put it away.
ben: Amazing. A mystery with no mystery.
mom: My favorite moment was hearing you two laugh while you folded the laundry. I almost didn't want to interrupt.
lucy: That was the missing-sock investigation. A very serious operation. Ben thinks for a moment, then looks toward the kitchen window.
ben: Professor Leaf has a new leaf. I noticed it this morning.
mom: Really? You'll have to show us after dinner.
lucy: We all turn to look at the little plant in its yellow pot. Ben stands up proudly, as if he grew the leaf himself. I realize nobody chose anything expensive or enormous. A seat, a tool, a laugh, a leaf.
dad: Now, who would like dessert?
ben: Finally. My next favorite moment is arriving.
lucy: We pass the plates around. I decide I like this game. Next week, I'll pay a little more attention to the tiny good things, especially the ones that happen before dessert.
